<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">tactics</ansi>

Company tactics are orders for your whole company: whom everyone goes for
(the focus), how hurt someone must be before your healers heal them in a
battle (the healing threshold), and how far they patch everyone up after it
(the patch threshold). They sit on top of each character's own strategy
(<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>), and are kept when you log out.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The focus</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">none</ansi>       each character goes for the foe its own strategy picks
  <ansi fg="command">leader</ansi>     their leader, the toughest of them
  <ansi fg="command">casters</ansi>    casters first: one chanting a spell first, then any caster,
             then the weakest foe once none is left
  <ansi fg="command">healers</ansi>    healers first: one chanting a heal first, then any healer
             (the weakest of them), then the casters order once none is left
  <ansi fg="command">nearest</ansi>    the front-most foe
  <ansi fg="command">weakest</ansi>    the foe with the least health left
  <ansi fg="command">strongest</ansi>  the foe with the most health left
  <ansi fg="command">wounded</ansi>    the most badly hurt foe, to finish it

<ansi fg="yellow">Until you set a focus, your company grows smarter with your level.</ansi> Its
default focus follows your level, as the enemy's coordination does:

  below level 10   <ansi fg="command">none</ansi>: each character by its own strategy
  level 10 and up  <ansi fg="command">weakest</ansi>: everyone goes for the foe with the least health
  level 25 and up  <ansi fg="command">casters</ansi>: casters first, then the weakest

<ansi fg="yellow">From level 5, a healer on the other side changes the default.</ansi> While you have
set no focus, your company goes for an enemy <ansi fg="command">healer</ansi> first whenever one
stands within reach, at any level from 5 up, and then goes back to the
level's default once the healer falls. A healer's heals can undo a whole
company's work, so the first blows go to it, a chanting one before an idle
one. Reach still comes first: whoever can't reach the healer keeps going
for the foe the level's default picks (or its own strategy).
<ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi> says when this default is in force, and the battle shows
"Your company marks the goblin shaman as a healer and goes for it first."

Setting a focus, <ansi fg="command">none</ansi> included, always wins over the default (and so does a
focus you call in a battle).
<ansi fg="command">company tactics focus default</ansi> goes back to the default for your level, and
<ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi> says when the default is in force. Also by default, the
first warrior among your companions <ansi fg="yellow">guards your healer</ansi> when it has no role
or ward of its own (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>).

With a focus set, everyone in your company, you included, goes for the
focus's choice instead of their own rule. Only whom they go for changes:
a healer still heals and a caster still casts, at the focus's choice.
Reach still counts: a fighter who can't reach the focus's choice goes for
the nearest foe it can reach (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Calling a focus in a battle</ansi>

A battle plays out on its own, but the focus is the one thing you can
change once it has begun. <ansi fg="command">company tactics focus [rule]</ansi> in a battle calls
it for that battle only:

  You call the company onto the bandit captain.

At the start of the next combat round, everyone who can reach the new
choice turns to it at once, even from a foe still standing; after that,
each keeps their target as usual. You can give one order a round: until
the company has turned, another is refused ("Your company is still
turning; try again next round."). <ansi fg="command">company tactics focus default</ansi> returns
to your saved focus. When the battle ends, the next one starts from your
saved focus again, and an order not yet carried out ends with it. Right
after <ansi fg="command">attack</ansi>, wait a round for the battle to begin before calling one. Nothing else about your tactics can change in a battle.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The healing threshold</ansi>

Healers heal anyone in your company below this share of their health
(of what their wounds allow, <ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>): 10 to 90 percent, in steps
of 10. It is 50, half health, until you change it. Someone down and
bleeding out is always healed first.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The patch threshold</ansi>

After a battle, and on <ansi fg="command">company patch</ansi>, your healers heal everyone up to this
share of their wound limit (<ansi fg="command">help patch</ansi>): 50 to 100 percent. It is
80, so the company starts its next fight nearly whole, and it is its own
setting: the healing threshold only governs healing in the middle of a
battle.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Guardians</ansi>

The focus decides whom your company strikes; a guardian decides who gets
struck. A guardian (a strategy role, set before a battle) steps in front
of its ward to take a blow meant for them, twice a battle (once more
every 10 levels), with a guard back every 2 rounds. A focus doesn't change whom a guardian guards
(<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Enemies have personalities too</ansi>

When an enemy picks a new target (it joins the fight, or its target falls
or is out of reach), it picks the way its kind does: wolves go for the
most badly hurt, the undead for the nearest, goblins for your healers and
casters, spiders and insects for the weakest, and people for the weakest.
Some foes chant spells: the goblin hexer croaks a Withering Hex at your
healers. A blow that draws blood may break a chant, so the <ansi fg="command">casters</ansi> focus
is how to stop them (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>).
Some are less predictable than others and now and then go for anyone
they can reach. How well a group fights together also counts: a rabble
picks wildly, a band or better turns on its leader's target, and a
drilled company goes for your casters first (<ansi fg="command">help coordination</ansi>).
<ansi fg="command">help targeting</ansi> has more.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi>                  your tactics and each member's strategy
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics focus [rule]</ansi>     set the focus (in a battle, for that battle)
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics focus default</ansi>    in a battle, back to your saved focus
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics healing [percent]</ansi> e.g. <ansi fg="command">company tactics healing 70</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics patch [percent]</ansi>  e.g. <ansi fg="command">company tactics patch 90</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics focus none</ansi>       keep every character on its own rule
  <ansi fg="command">company tactics default</ansi>          the level's default focus, healing 50, patch 80

<ansi fg="command">tactics</ansi> is short for <ansi fg="command">company tactics</ansi>. In the web client, the Combat tab
has a button for each focus during a battle, and your tactics under Setup
(<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help targeting</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>.

Clusters, open flanks, leaping enemies, and narrow ground change what your saved tactics can reach. See help battlefield.
